<div class="header"><h2>yeast extract and sodium can form MSG during cooking</h2></div>
<div class="body">

<p>The previous section noted that the FDA's <em>"monosodium glutamate"</em> labeling rule applies only when MSG has been <em>added</em> as an ingredient. This section follows that observation one step further: when a vendor adds a free-glutamate source such as <strong>yeast extract</strong> together with sodium-bearing ingredients (table salt, soy sauce, cheese, broth, etc.) and the dish is heated, the same MSG molecule can form <span class="highlight">during cooking</span> &mdash; without the vendor having ever added MSG.</p>

<h3>the FDA names yeast extract as a free-glutamate-plus-sodium carrier</h3>
<p>The FDA's own <em>Questions and Answers on Monosodium glutamate (MSG)</em> page, in its section "How can I know if my food contains MSG?", lists yeast extract among the ingredients in which the relevant glutamate + sodium pair is already present:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"[F]ree glutamate and sodium occur together naturally in ingredients such as hydrolyzed vegetable protein, autolyzed yeast, hydrolyzed yeast, <strong>yeast extract</strong>, soy extracts, and protein isolate, as well as in tomatoes and cheeses." <sup><a href="#src-fda">fda</a></sup></p>
</blockquote>
<p>The FDA does not require any of these ingredients to be labeled with their free-glutamate load:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"While FDA requires that these ingredients be listed on the ingredient panel, the agency does not require the label to also specify that they naturally contain glutamate." <sup><a href="#src-fda">fda</a></sup></p>
</blockquote>

<h3>why cooking can convert that pair into MSG</h3>
<p>Free L-glutamic acid (E620) is a weak acid. In a moist, heated food, it is in equilibrium with its salts. When sodium ions are present in the same matrix &mdash; from added salt, soy sauce, brined ingredients, cheese, or naturally occurring sodium in the broth &mdash; a fraction of the free glutamate pairs with sodium to form monosodium glutamate. The chemistry is not exotic: it is the same acid-base equilibrium that converts any glutamic-acid solution + sodium chloride into the glutamate salt. The Löï &amp; Cynober review documents that all five glutamate salts (E620 through E625) dissociate into the same free glutamate anion in aqueous solution, behaving identically to free glutamate in the body <sup><a href="#src-msg-alts-1">msg-alts-1</a></sup>; that reversibility is exactly what lets the glutamate ion <em>pick up</em> whichever cation is available in the cooking matrix.</p>
<p>The extent of the conversion depends on local concentrations, pH, water activity, and temperature &mdash; it is not 100% efficient &mdash; but the qualitative point is the same: a yeast-extract-containing dish cooked with sodium-bearing ingredients contains MSG that did not have to be declared as such.</p>

<h3>why the label still says nothing about this</h3>
<p>The FDA's labeling rule reads "monosodium glutamate" as an <em>added ingredient</em>. The agency does not regulate MSG that forms in situ during cooking from separately-declared components, because by the rule's own wording, none was added. The practical consequence: a label that lists "yeast extract" and "salt" can correspond to a finished dish whose free-glutamate-plus-sodium load meets or exceeds what the same dish would have if it had been seasoned with crystalline MSG. The vendor has done nothing the FDA forbids &mdash; the FDA simply does not require disclosure of the result.</p>

<h3>one honest caveat</h3>
<p><em>Once ingested, the glutamate ion from yeast-extract-plus-sodium is the same molecule as the glutamate ion from factory-made MSG.</em> The FDA's own page says so. This section makes a <em>labeling and chemistry</em> point: that "yeast extract" on an ingredient panel can correspond to a finished food that contains MSG, formed without MSG ever being declared. It is not a claim that in-situ-formed MSG behaves differently in the body from pre-mixed MSG. What <em>is</em> different is the labeling: a consumer who avoids "monosodium glutamate" on principle will not see this form of MSG named on the package.</p>

